-- title:
--   Killing all common eigenvectors forces nilpotence
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and $V$ a finite-dimensional $K$-vector space. Let $\iota$ be an index type and $t : \iota \to \operatorname{End}_K(V)$ a family of $K$-linear endomorphisms of $V$ which commute pairwise, i.e. $t_i t_j = t_j t_i$ for all $i, j$. Let $a \in \operatorname{End}_K(V)$ belong to the $K$-subalgebra $\operatorname{Algebra.adjoin} K (\operatorname{Set.range} t)$ of $\operatorname{End}_K(V)$ generated by the endomorphisms $t_i$. Assume that $a$ annihilates every common eigenvector of the family: for every function $\chi : \iota \to K$ and every $v \in V$ with $v \neq 0$ such that $t_i v = \chi_i \cdot v$ for all $i$, one has $a v = 0$. The conclusion is that $a$ is nilpotent, i.e. $a^m = 0$ for some natural number $m$. Note that the family $t$ is indexed by an arbitrary type, with no finiteness assumption on $\iota$, and that $\chi$ ranges over all functions $\iota \to K$, no consistency or algebraicity condition being imposed on it beyond the existence of the eigenvector $v$.
--
--   This is the standard passage from a statement about joint eigenvalue systems to a statement about annihilators: an element of a commutative algebra of commuting operators whose eigenvalue vanishes at every point of the joint spectrum lies in the Jacobson radical, hence is nilpotent. It is used in the mod $p$ modular forms part of the development, where it converts the assertion that every eigensystem occurring in a cohomology space already occurs in a given subspace into the nilpotence of an operator killing that subspace.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Module_End_isNilpotent_of_mem_adjoin_of_forall_eigenvector_apply_eq_zero.lean

